# loop/checks/secret-scan.allow · justified false positives for loop/checks/secret-scan.sh
#
# One entry per line. Blank lines and lines that start with # are ignored. "  # reason" may follow an entry.
#   <path-glob>:<rule>   ignore <rule> in files whose repo path matches the glob (fnmatch, * also crosses /).
#                        For a history finding the glob is also tried against <commit sha>:<path>, so
#                        <sha>*:<path>:<rule> accepts one commit's finding and nothing else.
#   <regex>              ignore a finding whose matched text matches the regex (Python re.search).
#                        Write one exact value per regex, never a range: a broad regex hides real hosts.
#
# secret-value is never allowlisted: an entry for it is an ERROR (exit 2). Remove the value instead.
# A regex never hides secret-value, fleet-ip, fleet-host or private-name, because it would have to spell
# the value it hides. Those take a <path-glob>:<rule> entry, and only after the holder accepts it.
#
# Keep it minimal. Every entry carries its reason. Never put a real address, host, key or value here:
# this file is public too, and the scan reads it.

# specs/attendance.md §7.3 fixture "Not allowlisted" and the sign-in policy tests: a consumer address that must be refused.
^(someone|a)@gmail\.com$
# specs/attendance.md §6.4: the example value of BOOTSTRAP_OWNER_EMAIL.
^owner@company\.com$

# IP unit tests (apps/attendance/tests/unit/ip*.test.ts): public addresses next to the documentation and
# CGNAT ranges, used to test parsing and range edges. None of them is a server.
^1\.2\.3\.4(/[0-9]+)?$
^8\.8\.8\.0/24$
^198\.51\.101\.1$
^100\.(63\.255|128\.0)\.0/24$
^100\.128\.0\.1$

# Tailnet unit tests (D-003, ALLOW_TAILNET_CIDRS): made-up CGNAT and Tailscale ULA hosts, one value each.
# None of them is a node of the fleet (a fleet address is reported as fleet-ip, which a regex cannot hide).
^100\.101\.102\.7/24$
^100\.127\.255\.255/16$
^100\.101\.102\.103$
^100\.90\.12\.34(/32)?$
^fd7a:115c:a1e0::1234$

# The holder's first prompt, quoted verbatim in docs/history, calls the orchestrator by a role word that is also
# a fleet host name. It names a role, not a machine. Scoped to these two files only.
docs/history/01-human-prompts.md:fleet-host
docs/history/data/human-messages.jsonl:fleet-host
